<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The Phoenix actions add-on for the company's own Headwind MDM server.

  🔴 THIS IS NOT BUILT BY THE PHOENIX REPOSITORY'S CI. The six required checks are TypeScript and do
  not see this directory. It is compiled by the Docker build that produces the Headwind image, against
  the Headwind jars taken out of the pinned WAR itself — which is why every Headwind dependency below
  is `provided` scope rather than downloaded: the versions must be the ones the running server
  actually has, and pulling a different one from a repository is how a plugin compiles cleanly and
  then fails to load.

  🔴 EVERY VERSION BELOW IS MEASURED FROM `hmdm-5.40.1-os.war`, NOT CHOSEN. Measured 2026-09-26 by
  unpacking it: 114 jars in WEB-INF/lib, 73 loose classes in WEB-INF/classes.

    com.hmdm:common:0.1.0    `SyncResponseHook`, `SyncResponseInt` and the `Sync*Int` types the
                             interface's generics name; also `AuthFilter`, `PrivateIPFilter`,
                             `PublicIPFilter`.
    com.hmdm:platform:0.1.0  `PluginConfiguration` — the interface Headwind's classpath scan looks
                             for, and the only reason this add-on is ever instantiated.
    com.hmdm:jwt:0.2.0       `JWTFilter`, the first filter on the private path.

  There is no `com.hmdm:server` artifact and never was: Headwind's core is eleven small jars named for
  their area, all at 0.1.0, and the REST layer that consumes them is loose classes in WEB-INF/classes
  rather than a jar at all. An earlier revision of this file depended on `server`, the Dockerfile's
  `find -name 'server*.jar'` matched nothing, and Maven answered "Is a directory" with an empty
  version in the coordinate.

  The rest are `provided` too, at the versions the WAR actually ships rather than the newest that
  resolves: guice 4.2.2 (not 4.2.3), jackson-annotations 2.10.0 (not 2.13.4), javax.ws.rs-api 2.0.1
  (not 2.1.1). Compiling against a NEWER provided API than the server carries is the one mistake in
  this file that would not fail the build — it fails at runtime, once, as a NoSuchMethodError inside a
  hook nobody is watching.

  🔴 `javax.servlet-api` IS THE ONE VERSION NOT READ OUT OF THE WAR, BECAUSE IT IS NOT IN THE WAR.
  The servlet API is provided by Tomcat, and the pinned image is `tomcat:9-jdk11-temurin-jammy`, which
  is Servlet 4.0 — hence 4.0.1. It is used only as the declared type of a parameter this add-on passes
  straight through, so the risk a wrong version would carry is nil, but the reason is written down so
  the next reader does not go looking for it in the WAR and conclude something is missing.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <groupId>com.satopharma.phoenix</groupId>
  <artifactId>headwind-phoenix-actions</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>
  <name>Phoenix actions for Headwind MDM</name>

  <properties>
    <!--
      🔴 JAVA 8 BYTECODE, AND IT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE — A JAVA 11 BUILD TAKES THE WHOLE SERVER DOWN.
      This said 11, and the fourth and fifth Railway builds both compiled, deployed, and started a
      container Railway called SUCCESS while the application served nothing at all:

          SEVERE StandardContext.filterStart Exception starting filter [HMDMApplication]
            java.lang.IllegalArgumentException
              at jersey.repackaged.org.objectweb.asm.ClassReader.<init>(ClassReader.java:171)
              at org.glassfish.jersey.server.internal.scanning.AnnotationAcceptingListener.process
              at org.glassfish.jersey.server.ResourceConfig.scanClasses
          SEVERE StandardContext.startInternal Context [] startup failed due to previous errors

      Headwind runs **Jersey 2.25.1**, which scans every jar in WEB-INF/lib for @Path using a
      repackaged **ASM 5**. ASM 5 reads class files up to major 52 and throws a bare
      IllegalArgumentException — no message, no class name — on anything newer. Java 11 emits major
      55. So the add-on did not merely fail to load: Jersey aborted its scan, the HMDMApplication
      filter failed to start, and Tomcat killed the entire context. **A working Headwind server was
      broken by a compiler flag.**

      Measured 2026-09-26 from `hmdm-5.40.1-os.war`: every one of Headwind's own 255 classes is
      major 52 — common, platform, jwt, messaging and the 73 loose ones in WEB-INF/classes, without
      a single exception. Matching them is the requirement.

      `release` rather than `source`/`target`: those two compile old syntax against the JDK 11
      class library, so a Java 11-only method resolves at build time and NoSuchMethodErrors at
      runtime. `release` pins the API as well. Pinned by tests/headwind-bridge.test.ts.
    -->
    <maven.compiler.release>8</maven.compiler.release>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    <!-- Overridden by the Dockerfile from each jar's own filename, so an image upgrade needs no edit
         here. The defaults are what 5.40.1 ships. -->
    <hmdm.common.version>0.1.0</hmdm.common.version>
    <hmdm.platform.version>0.1.0</hmdm.platform.version>
    <hmdm.jwt.version>0.2.0</hmdm.jwt.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.hmdm</groupId>
      <artifactId>common</artifactId>
      <version>${hmdm.common.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.hmdm</groupId>
      <artifactId>platform</artifactId>
      <version>${hmdm.platform.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.hmdm</groupId>
      <artifactId>jwt</artifactId>
      <version>${hmdm.jwt.version}</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.google.inject</groupId>
      <artifactId>guice</artifactId>
      <version>4.2.2</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- `ServletModule`, the base class of PhoenixRestModule. Separate artifact from guice itself. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.google.inject.extensions</groupId>
      <artifactId>guice-servlet</artifactId>
      <version>4.2.2</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>javax.servlet</groupId>
      <artifactId>javax.servlet-api</artifactId>
      <version>4.0.1</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>javax.ws.rs</groupId>
      <artifactId>javax.ws.rs-api</artifactId>
      <version>2.0.1</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
      <artifactId>jackson-annotations</artifactId>
      <version>2.10.0</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>javax.inject</groupId>
      <artifactId>javax.inject</artifactId>
      <version>1</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <finalName>headwind-phoenix-actions</finalName>
  </build>
</project>
